                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose: programme logic & partner landscape`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The board has greenlit a <strong>14-month Executive Master in International Management</strong> on a fixed <strong>€1.2M</strong> budget, to break even at <strong>45 executives</strong> by cohort 2. Before you rank any partner, articulate what an international master must <em>teach</em> to be credible across regions, and state which capability ABS must keep in-house versus source from a partner. Three candidates are on the table — a prestige US school, a strong-fit Singapore school, a high-demand Brazilian school — plus Lumière’s offer to co-own everything. Score them on the partner-fit rubric; a name is not a strategy.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Plan: partner selection & the coopetition boundary`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Choose one or two partners and <em>draw the coopetition boundary with Lumière</em>: which assets to pool (the shared Singapore recruitment office, logistics) and which to wall off (<strong>pricing, the candidate pipeline, faculty IP</strong>). The cheapest path — full co-ownership — is also the one that most erodes your defensible position: pool everything and you hand a rival visibility into the very candidates you both chase. Keep total revenue-share commitments inside the <strong>€1.2M</strong> envelope. If you bring Brazil in, decide now how you treat its accreditation gap.`,
