                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_designed:      `<strong>That’s the right starting move.</strong> A credible international master is built around calendar reconciliation, multi-campus logistics and culturally aligned content — not a French course with foreign postcodes. Design the value proposition first and the partner rubric writes itself.`,
                    coach_r1_export:        `<strong>“A French master delivered abroad” is the export trap.</strong> An international programme is purpose-built, not shipped. Treat the “international” part as logistics and your cultural-alignment score sinks — and so does completion in your partner markets.`,
                    coach_r1_cheapest:      `<strong>Letting the budget write the value proposition inverts the logic.</strong> Decide what the programme must be, then find partners who can deliver it inside €1.2M. Chasing the cheapest mix first usually buys a thin brand nobody pays €32,000 for.`,
                    coach_r1_keepbrand:     `<strong>“Buy brand, let the partner lead” surrenders your core on day one.</strong> If a partner owns your pricing, pipeline and faculty IP, you are renting prestige and giving away your competitive position. Decide what stays in-house <em>before</em> you negotiate.`,
                    coach_r1_fitbrand:      `<strong>Brand-first ranking is exactly the trap the rubric exists to prevent.</strong> Prestige is a cost as well as a benefit — the most prestigious name here demands €9,000 per student. Weight brand against cost, fit, accreditation and cultural distance.`,
                    coach_r1_fitcost:       `<strong>Cost-first ranking protects the envelope and starves demand.</strong> The cheapest partner usually carries the thinnest brand — and nobody pays €32,000 for a badge they don’t recognise. Cost is one dimension of the rubric, not the rubric.`,
                    coach_r1_norigour:      `<strong>Light on the economics this early.</strong> If you don’t model partner costs into a cohort P&L now, you’ll discover the US revenue share or the Brazil sunk-cost risk too late. Rigour up front is cheaper than a surprise in December.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You defined a purpose-built international master and named pricing, pipeline and faculty IP as the core ABS keeps in-house. That’s the frame that lets you collaborate with a coopetitor without surrendering your position.`,
                    coach_r2_us_trap:       `<strong>Beautiful logo on the diploma — now run the P&L.</strong> Keystone’s €9,000-per-student share is <em>€405,000 a year at 45 students</em>, and your contribution margin just went negative. Prestige bought without modelling its cost is the brand trap — break-even moves out a whole cohort.`,
                    coach_r2_us_modelled:   `<strong>You took the prestige brand with your eyes open.</strong> You modelled the €9,000-per-student share before signing, so you know break-even moved and you can price for it. Prestige as a deliberate, costed choice is defensible — prestige as a reflex is not.`,
                    coach_r2_sg_only:       `<strong>Best fit and cost, thinnest brand — watch the demand side.</strong> Marina alone keeps the envelope healthy, but a single unfamiliar badge struggles to fill 45 seats at €32,000. A second, stronger partner — or real marketing — is what lifts enrolment past break-even.`,
                    coach_r2_sg_us:         `<strong>Fit-and-cost anchor plus a prestige co-brand — a strong architecture.</strong> Marina carries the calendar and cost fit; Keystone carries the brand that fills seats. Just keep the US revenue share inside your P&L so the prestige stays a costed choice, not a surprise.`,
                    coach_r2_sg_br:         `<strong>Fit-and-cost anchor plus regional demand — promising, with one landmine.</strong> Marina and Cordilheira together can lift enrolment, but Brazil’s accreditation gap is live. De-risk it now or the December panel can turn €740K into sunk cost.`,
                    coach_r2_br_only:       `<strong>Cheapest path, real regional demand, real accreditation risk.</strong> Cordilheira alone is a bet that the documentation clears. If you haven’t de-risked the gap, you’re one rejection away from a sunk launch — price that risk before you commit.`,
                    coach_r2_bound_good:    `<strong>That’s the coopetition boundary drawn on purpose.</strong> Pool the shared Singapore office and logistics — real, cheap collaboration — and wall off pricing, pipeline and faculty IP. You get the cost benefit of the alliance without handing Lumière the assets that define your position.`,
                    coach_r2_coown:         `<strong>You pooled everything to halve your costs.</strong> Six months in, Lumière’s recruiters use the shared Singapore office to steer the strongest candidates into <em>their</em> parallel programme at a 10% lower price. You handed your rival your pricing and your pipeline — the competitive core just collapsed and enrolment is leaking to Lyon.`,
                    coach_r2_exit:          `<strong>Exiting the alliance protects the core — and costs you reach.</strong> Walling everything off keeps Lumière out of your pricing and pipeline, but you also gave up the shared Singapore recruitment office. The boundary isn’t all-or-nothing: the skill is pooling the office while protecting the core.`,
                    coach_r2_brazil_ignore: `<strong>You brought Brazil in and left the accreditation gap unpriced.</strong> The regional demand is real, but so is the December panel. Assume-it-clears is how launch spend becomes sunk cost — decide your fallback before €740K is committed.`,
                    coach_r2_brazil_derisk: `<strong>De-risking the Brazil gap now is cheap insurance.</strong> A contingency review and a fallback mean a December rejection costs you a review fee, not €740K and a year. That’s pricing accreditation risk into the partner choice — exactly the discipline the rubric rewards.`,
                    coach_r3_impose:        `<strong>“Partners adapt to our calendar” is export thinking.</strong> A cross-border programme reconciles French, partner and learner calendars — impose one and the partner cohorts hit clashes, engagement drops and your cultural-alignment score falls. Reconcile, don’t dictate.`,
                    coach_r3_capitulate:    `<strong>Handing the partner whatever calendar it wants protects the relationship and bruises your faculty.</strong> Capitulation isn’t reconciliation — French faculty availability is a constraint to design around, not to sacrifice. Find the calendar that works across all three, not just the loudest partner.`,
                    coach_r3_surface:       `<strong>A surface layer helps, but translation isn’t alignment.</strong> Translated slides and a guest speaker don’t fix cases, grading and assessment that still assume a French context. Designed-in cross-cultural pedagogy is what moves completion in the partner cohorts.`,
                    coach_r3_culnone:       `<strong>Your cultural-alignment score is heading for the floor.</strong> French cases, French grading, French assumptions — the Brazilian cohort reports the whole experience assumes a French context, and projected completion drops to the low 60s. That’s an export, not an international master.`,
                    coach_r3_optimistic:    `<strong>You built the P&L to break even at 58.</strong> Your own analyst’s range is 30–60 — a programme that only works at the top of the forecast fails at the midpoint. What partner or pricing change makes <em>45</em> the break-even, not 58?`,
                    coach_r3_conservative:  `<strong>Break-even planned at 45 — the conservative midpoint.</strong> That’s the number the analyst flagged and the board mandated. Build the cohort P&L to clear 45 and the upside of a strong cohort is a bonus, not a lifeline.`,
                    coach_r3_defensive:     `<strong>Planning to break even at 32 is prudent — perhaps too prudent.</strong> Designing for the bottom of the range protects you on the downside, but a programme that only needs 32 to clear may be under-built on brand and ambition. 45 is the mandated target; aim there.`,
                    coach_r3_lowmkt:        `<strong>Thin marketing on a new international badge.</strong> Even a strong partner mix needs demand generation to fill 45 seats at €32,000. Under-spend on the launch and enrolment stalls below break-even — the under-investment failure mode.`,
                    coach_r4_rush:          `<strong>A thin package submitted on time is a coin toss.</strong> Accreditation panels reject incomplete partner documentation — and a rejection this late sinks your launch. On-time only counts if it’s also complete.`,
                    coach_r4_late:          `<strong>It’s December and your documentation is late.</strong> Miss the deadline and the launch slips a full year, with most of the €1.2M already committed. Quality matters — but the deadline is the constraint, and you blew through it.`,
                    coach_r4_brazilsunk:    `<strong>It’s December. The panel just rejected the partner documentation.</strong> Brazil’s local accreditation isn’t recognised under your framework, the gap you didn’t price is now real, launch slips to 2028 and <em>€740K is sunk</em>. Was that risk priced into your Round 2 choice?`,
                    coach_r4_conditional:   `<strong>A conditional go is honest risk management.</strong> Launching only once the open risk — enrolment, accreditation or the boundary — is closed protects the €1.2M and the Dean’s mandate. It’s the recommendation a board trusts.`,
                    coach_r4_go:            `<strong>A clean go — if the numbers back it.</strong> Funded inside €1.2M, ≥45 enrolments, the core protected and accreditation in hand is exactly the case the Dean greenlit. Make sure all four are true before you say the word.`,
                    coach_r4_forcego:       `<strong>“We’ve spent too much to stop” is the sunk-cost fallacy wearing a launch plan.</strong> Money already committed is gone whether you launch or not. Pushing a programme live <em>because</em> of what you spent — not because it works — is how a bad bet becomes a worse one.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>That defends the boundary the way a board trusts.</strong> Naming what you pooled, what you walled off, and why the protected core is intact shows the coopetition was deliberate, not accidental. You collaborated with a rival without surrendering your position.`,
                    coach_r4_defbrand:      `<strong>Letting the brand answer for the strategy dodges the question.</strong> The Dean asked about the coopetition boundary, not the logo. A prestigious partner doesn’t protect your pricing or your pipeline — only a deliberate boundary does.`,
                    coach_r4_defcost:       `<strong>“Look how much we saved” treats the boundary as a detail.</strong> The savings are exactly why the question matters: the cheapest alliance is the one that leaks the most. Defend what you protected, not what you spent.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
