                    insight_launch_ready:   `Launch approved: you built a purpose-built international master, chose a partner architecture that fills 45 seats inside the €1.2M envelope, and drew the coopetition boundary on purpose — pooling the Singapore office while walling off pricing, pipeline and faculty IP. The programme is fundable, breaks even at the conservative midpoint, is culturally aligned and accredited on time, and your competitive core is intact.`,
                    insight_boundary:       `Boundary surrendered: full co-ownership with Lumière halved your costs and handed your rival your pricing and your candidate pipeline. Six months in, the shared Singapore office is steering your strongest candidates into Lumière’s parallel programme — the cheapest alliance was the one that most eroded your position. The competitive core, not the budget, was the asset that mattered.`,
                    insight_accreditation:  `Sunk by accreditation: a partner with an unresolved accreditation gap, an unpriced risk and a missed (or thin) December package converted launch spend into sunk cost. €740K is committed, the launch slips a full year, and the Dean’s mandate has evaporated — a real-options reason to value a good-enough, low-risk partner over a prestigious, high-risk one.`,
                    insight_brand:          `Prestige over profit: you bought the most prestigious partner without modelling its €9,000-per-student revenue share against break-even. At 45 students that share alone is €405,000 a year — contribution margin went negative and break-even moved out a full cohort. Prestige is a cost, not just a benefit.`,
                    insight_export:         `Exported, not designed: the programme was built as a French master delivered abroad rather than a purpose-built international one. The cultural-alignment score stayed low, partner-cohort completion suffered, and the “international” credential never felt credible in its partner markets.`,
                    insight_underinvest:    `Under-invested: you played safe on cost with a single thin-brand partner and thin marketing, and enrolment came in below break-even because nobody pays €32,000 for an unknown badge. Under-investment is also a failure mode — a second, stronger partner or real demand generation was what would have lifted enrolment past 45.`,
                    insight_optimistic:     `Optimistic enrolment: your cohort P&L was built to break even near the top of the analyst’s 30–60 range, so the programme fails at the midpoint of its own forecast. Conservative enrolment planning — break-even at 45, not 58 — is what makes the launch robust to demand uncertainty.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: parts of the design landed, but the partner choice, the coopetition boundary, the cultural alignment and the enrolment plan didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough open seams for the Dean to question whether the launch is robust.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You breached the €1.2M envelope: the budget was a hard cap, and a programme that costs more than the board funded is not launchable as scoped. Trim partner guarantees or marketing back inside the envelope.`,
                    insight_belowbe:        `Projected enrolment is below the 45-student break-even: at this volume the cohort does not cover its costs, and the path to black ink by cohort 2 is not credible.`,
                    insight_align_low:      `Cultural alignment stayed low: without designed-in cross-cultural pedagogy and reconciled calendars, completion in the partner cohorts is at risk and the international credential reads as an export.`,
                    insight_core_low:       `Your competitive core is exposed: pricing, pipeline or faculty IP were not fully protected, leaving the door open for a partner — especially the coopetitor — to erode your position.`,
                    insight_cps_high:       `Cost per student is high: the launch amortisation and any per-student revenue share are pushing your unit economics well above the €26,667 break-even reference — fragile margins for cohort 2.`,

                    hint_boundary:          `You’ve ceded the coopetition boundary — Lumière now sees your pricing and pipeline and enrolment is leaking to Lyon. The competitive core, once surrendered, is hard to recover; protect what’s left and own the trade-off to the Dean.`,
                    hint_accreditation:     `The accreditation gap has sunk part of your launch spend. Stabilise with a complete on-time package and a conditional go — pushing live regardless only compounds the sunk cost.`,
                    hint_overbudget:        `You’re over the €1.2M cap. Trim a partner guarantee or the marketing spend back inside the envelope before the go/no-go.`,
                    hint_brand:             `The prestige partner’s €9,000-per-student share has pushed break-even out and enrolment is short. Either price for it, add a fit-and-cost anchor, or reconsider the brand trap.`,
                    hint_align:             `Cultural alignment is dangerously low — you’re building an export, not an international master. Reconcile the calendars and design cross-cultural pedagogy in before completion suffers.`,
                    hint_optimistic:        `Your break-even is built at the top of the demand range. Re-plan for a 45-student break-even so the programme survives the midpoint of its own forecast.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — funded, above break-even, aligned and the core protected. Land it: a complete accreditation package, a disciplined go decision and owning the boundary will hold it before the Dean.`,

